{
  "families": [
    {
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 12, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 10, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "m", "low": 2, "high": 8, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 1.0,
      "why": "pipelined_m can trade Fmax for fewer pipeline registers by increasing m, directly reducing flip-flop count and thus luts_plus_ffs. Since the throughput constraint (>=1 MSPS) is very loose for a small CORDIC (1 result/cycle, Fmax expected well above 1 MHz), larger m is likely beneficial. The ranges cover the plausible accuracy region: data_width >=12 is needed because output LSB = 2^-(W-2) and max_abs_err <= 2^-10; n_iter >=10 covers the CORDIC angle-resolution threshold. Full guard-bit and rounding ranges let the search find the minimal area for each accuracy level. m from 2..8 explores the area/Fmax trade-off while ensuring feasible points if large m violates timing."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec requires >=1 MSPS and max_abs_err <= 2^-10 while minimising luts_plus_ffs. Both pipelined families produce one result per cycle, so throughput equals Fmax; for these small CORDIC designs Fmax is expected to be far above 1 MHz, making the throughput constraint non-binding. The real trade-off is area vs accuracy, controlled by data_width, n_iter, angle_guard, frac_guard and rounding. pipelined_m strictly generalises pipelined by allowing m>1, which reduces the number of pipeline registers (FFs) without changing the combinational logic or accuracy. Therefore pipelined (m=1) is expected to be dominated in luts_plus_ffs for any given accuracy, so the entire budget is allocated to pipelined_m to map the Pareto front thoroughly. The chosen ranges span the feasible accuracy region and include the low-area corner (small W, small N, large m) as well as higher-accuracy points to extend the front."
}
